[{:db/id 17592186087779 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-9012ef7a-2685-5e88-afc5-3c045fe01d15" "question-ac410cb2-42ca-5768-b913-c9a0282fb269"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087606 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n![](images/67/67ddf1272badaf7af1e13ad44728bbcc20554dd60974939deec57b4d68e1d95d.svg)" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the complementary solution by solving the associated homogeneous ODE. The characteristic equation has roots ![](images/fc/fc98374a630d6269fbeaa595a26657a2a2a93c4fa54325ef1fa95e6f648bc5d3.svg) so the complementary solution is\n![](images/fd/fd5408e519fbfc48ea58ba46384bf4f9ece51a68ac3488bf9ac03a7978d06cd4.svg)\n\nSecond, we find a particular solution of the inhomogeneous ODE. We see that the right-hand side is ![](images/ee/ee4cb113b8bd85252077b002ac0ae9e170bc04975254ed47385ab98104c7c8e6.svg) and ![](images/c2/c228482a36b98d24cb5db669b2ea5dbc6ba571979e4da7a7c80a77311a9a7de8.svg) is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form\n![](images/c3/c37a270e8e9982b6606729b52e4a3b9e3aff50e809545aae065e502c66b1f3ac.svg)\nwhere ![](images/79/7995b2daef1650484d2fa6518cb775356ae12ecdf597eba9e2e04f4f35dee93c.svg) and ![](images/f2/f27acf6959fc15846a7239dae3b1f8398bc7a1129e9bdf8175acc9476f7cbca0.svg) are constants to be determined.\n\n**Caution:** The terms of the complementary solution are ![](images/7e/7e6f0ca90c220b0a5cdef6c9340b306c4046de68f8652b17b7eba46d9b108d52.svg) and ![](images/9d/9d721df1e9ef99b68b600a35c96c1018191bb61c2722ef055174d33cfabee6cf.svg) Although ![](images/c2/c228482a36b98d24cb5db669b2ea5dbc6ba571979e4da7a7c80a77311a9a7de8.svg) appears, ![](images/c2/c228482a36b98d24cb5db669b2ea5dbc6ba571979e4da7a7c80a77311a9a7de8.svg) is not itself a term because it is multiplied by ![](images/d3/d34ab740163cb05013fa9215e5477416d899d8019bce5ac7af648477625b014a.svg)\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ![](images/4d/4d58e4bb5a428c380f2260e2338284a14084a2ec5dc1a88a8c7ac110e8e39c93.svg) we get\n![](images/8b/8b95b69c723d32b6decc46879f28b91149711ddf36c951afcd4bca584d562819.svg)\n\nTo find the values of ![](images/79/7995b2daef1650484d2fa6518cb775356ae12ecdf597eba9e2e04f4f35dee93c.svg) and ![](images/e5/e5f9648e0ed288008f7cba215426334563f09457530f9479a3ebd4918dca76d2.svg) we substitute the derivatives into the original ODE and get\n![](images/9c/9cd2ec7928ef4dde85caf25d3fb2c9e0a3cee570e7e9b39bb263f8f9dd45a000.svg)\n\nThe corresponding terms on both sides should have the same coefficients. Equating the coefficients of like terms, we get the following system:\n![](images/6b/6b227d6734ad4d87d42fb445a79396a2f68ed896f5baad98e74f6347c45d7245.svg)\n\nSolving this system gives ![](images/a4/a4ee605afe3f68a010f06c3343aff9d7f967a3b3fd68c5ad5caae3695b745910.svg)\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n![](images/5a/5a44cc55479bd8dd9c33482fdeb61eb6d2a2720bfe0abcca95cd8b2a9ba58dba.svg)\nand the general solution is\n![](images/74/74724ed0976cafdb18816dc3146552dee7ebbe4746fb01cf30858805facdeee1.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087780 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-1961edb1-4065-5e30-966d-35cef05a7365"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087607 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the complementary solution by solving the associated homogeneous ODE. The characteristic equation has roots $\\lambda = \\pm 2 \\textrm{i},$ so the complementary solution is \n\n$$\ny_c = A\\cos{2x} + B \\sin{2x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution $y_p(x).$\n\nSecond, we find a particular solution of the inhomogeneous ODE. We see that the right-hand side is $4 \\sin 2x,$ and $\\sin 2x$ is a term in the complementary solution. So, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p = x (\\alpha \\cos{2x} + \\beta \\sin{2x}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x} & =(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+2x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x), \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =4(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)+4x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute the derivatives into the original ODE and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)+4x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+4x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x) & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\ -4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}4\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x) \\\\ -4\\alpha =4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha = -1$ and $\\beta = 0.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = -x \\cos{2x},\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={y}_{c}+{y}_{p} \\\\  & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-9012ef7a-2685-5e88-afc5-3c045fe01d15" :question/id #uuid "9012ef7a-2685-5e88-afc5-3c045fe01d15" :question/math-academy-id "q-64273" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2y=10\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{-x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $10\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ So we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-2(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=10\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(-3\\alpha -\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+(\\alpha -3\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=10\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}-3\\alpha -\\beta =10\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ \\alpha -3\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-3$ and $\\beta =-1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7437ed1b-b3b5-570b-9f04-43fb6c608cb0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7437ed1b-b3b5-570b-9f04-43fb6c608cb0" :answer-field/id #uuid "7437ed1b-b3b5-570b-9f04-43fb6c608cb0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e916876a-ab3b-5e56-a063-0f250160ca0c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e916876a-ab3b-5e56-a063-0f250160ca0c" "answer-242d2d52-f50c-5e80-9ccb-b96c31a527b3" "answer-e8bccbd1-e170-5521-a778-3b5dc8e5cf86" "answer-5ab46f34-d8f7-59d6-bdbd-52cb67a2e827" "answer-ced8ae93-61ba-5589-ac22-bbbff77d9eef"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e916876a-ab3b-5e56-a063-0f250160ca0c" :answer/id #uuid "e916876a-ab3b-5e56-a063-0f250160ca0c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-242d2d52-f50c-5e80-9ccb-b96c31a527b3" :answer/id #uuid "242d2d52-f50c-5e80-9ccb-b96c31a527b3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e8bccbd1-e170-5521-a778-3b5dc8e5cf86" :answer/id #uuid "e8bccbd1-e170-5521-a778-3b5dc8e5cf86" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}_{p}=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5ab46f34-d8f7-59d6-bdbd-52cb67a2e827" :answer/id #uuid "5ab46f34-d8f7-59d6-bdbd-52cb67a2e827" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}_{p}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ced8ae93-61ba-5589-ac22-bbbff77d9eef" :answer/id #uuid "ced8ae93-61ba-5589-ac22-bbbff77d9eef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}_{p}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ac410cb2-42ca-5768-b913-c9a0282fb269" :question/id #uuid "ac410cb2-42ca-5768-b913-c9a0282fb269" :question/math-academy-id "q-8121" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+5y=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+5y=0\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +5 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +1)-1+5 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2} & =-4 \\\\ \\lambda +1 & =\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-1\\pm 2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-1\\pm 2\\text{i}$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}={e}^{-x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x).\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+2(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+5(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives:\n\n$$\n(4\\alpha +2\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+(-2\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}4\\alpha +2\\beta =6\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ -2\\alpha +4\\beta =2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =1$ and $\\beta =1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-73960a55-87e7-51f7-88d8-3235589147ec"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-73960a55-87e7-51f7-88d8-3235589147ec" :answer-field/id #uuid "73960a55-87e7-51f7-88d8-3235589147ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e6867f40-4226-51fd-bc11-e7fdeae8d0a4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a9935964-0a92-5948-9e9b-226df2fc5db4" "answer-e6867f40-4226-51fd-bc11-e7fdeae8d0a4" "answer-3ae0d677-84f2-52e7-8b0c-cac1d9bc44ce" "answer-35e4d2e5-706c-5258-9456-9e4bb7705e64" "answer-1c44fa17-bdc5-51f4-a438-7e8acea31ae7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a9935964-0a92-5948-9e9b-226df2fc5db4" :answer/id #uuid "a9935964-0a92-5948-9e9b-226df2fc5db4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y={e}^{-x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e6867f40-4226-51fd-bc11-e7fdeae8d0a4" :answer/id #uuid "e6867f40-4226-51fd-bc11-e7fdeae8d0a4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y={e}^{-x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3ae0d677-84f2-52e7-8b0c-cac1d9bc44ce" :answer/id #uuid "3ae0d677-84f2-52e7-8b0c-cac1d9bc44ce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y={e}^{-x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-35e4d2e5-706c-5258-9456-9e4bb7705e64" :answer/id #uuid "35e4d2e5-706c-5258-9456-9e4bb7705e64" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y={e}^{-x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+x(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1c44fa17-bdc5-51f4-a438-7e8acea31ae7" :answer/id #uuid "1c44fa17-bdc5-51f4-a438-7e8acea31ae7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y={e}^{-x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+x(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186335696 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =\\pm \\text{i}$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe particular solution of the inhomogeneous equation cannot be of the form $\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ because this is already included in the complementary function. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\ny_p = x (\\alpha \\cos x + \\beta \\sin x),\n]\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & =2(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n2(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ -2\\alpha =8\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-4$ and $\\beta =0.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-4x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-1961edb1-4065-5e30-966d-35cef05a7365" :question/id #uuid "1961edb1-4065-5e30-966d-35cef05a7365" :question/math-academy-id "q-64274" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+4y=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "The particular solution of the inhomogeneous equation cannot be of the form $\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ because this is already included in the complementary function. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x} & =(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+2x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =4(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)-4x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n4(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)-4x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+4x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}4\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x) \\\\ -4\\alpha =-4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =1$ and $\\beta =0.$ Therefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d0af414f-f694-5afc-9853-4520f38d8893"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d0af414f-f694-5afc-9853-4520f38d8893" :answer-field/id #uuid "d0af414f-f694-5afc-9853-4520f38d8893" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-57a9a361-15d8-51a5-8e9b-65d82be746f1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2dba37db-2930-5acd-8ac5-1cb53904e50c" "answer-57a9a361-15d8-51a5-8e9b-65d82be746f1" "answer-23ff9445-e0ef-5464-9534-037aa0779f66" "answer-a2852e49-1123-5dbc-9513-0f9543e796a2" "answer-444f2f73-423e-52cf-8993-630d12d4bc82"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2dba37db-2930-5acd-8ac5-1cb53904e50c" :answer/id #uuid "2dba37db-2930-5acd-8ac5-1cb53904e50c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-57a9a361-15d8-51a5-8e9b-65d82be746f1" :answer/id #uuid "57a9a361-15d8-51a5-8e9b-65d82be746f1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}_{p}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-23ff9445-e0ef-5464-9534-037aa0779f66" :answer/id #uuid "23ff9445-e0ef-5464-9534-037aa0779f66" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}_{p}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a2852e49-1123-5dbc-9513-0f9543e796a2" :answer/id #uuid "a2852e49-1123-5dbc-9513-0f9543e796a2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}_{p}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-444f2f73-423e-52cf-8993-630d12d4bc82" :answer/id #uuid "444f2f73-423e-52cf-8993-630d12d4bc82" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}_{p}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186348249 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-{y}^{′}-2y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -2 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)(\\lambda -2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-1$ or $\\lambda =2$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {y}^{″} & =-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x-(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-2(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives:\n\n$$\n(-3\\alpha -\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+(\\alpha -3\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}-3\\alpha -\\beta =3\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ \\alpha -3\\beta =-1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-1,$ $\\beta =0.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{2x}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nNow let us find constants $A$ and $B.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(0)=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA+B-1=0.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=-1.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-A{e}^{-x}+2B{e}^{2x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=-1$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n-A+2B=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations for $A$ and $B$:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}A+B=1 \\\\ -A+2B=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=1,$ $B=0.$\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-x}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186088955 :tutorial/content "Let's solve the following second-order ODE with sinusoidal forcing:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} + \\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} - 2y= 2 \\cos 2 x\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution by solving the associated homogeneous ODE. The characteristic equation (or auxiliary equation) is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -2 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda +2) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso $\\lambda = 1$ or $\\lambda = -2.$ Consequently, the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c = Ae^{x} + Be^{- 2 x}.\n$$\n\nSecond, we find a particular solution of the inhomogeneous ODE. We see that the right-hand side is $2 \\cos 2x,$ and $\\cos 2x$ does not appear as a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form\n\n$$\ny_p =\\alpha \\cos 2x + \\beta  \\sin 2x\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x} & =-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ \\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =-4\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-4\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute the derivatives into the original ODE and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-4\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-2(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ (2\\beta -6\\alpha )\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+(-6\\beta -2\\alpha )\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding terms on both sides should have the same coefficients. Equating the coefficients of like terms, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2\\beta -6\\alpha =2 \\\\ -6\\beta -2\\alpha =0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, it follows that \n\n$$\n- 6 \\beta - 2 \\alpha = 0 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\alpha = - 3 \\beta.\n$$\n\n Substituting $\\alpha=-3\\beta$ into the first equation gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\beta -6(-3)\\beta  & =2 \\\\ 20\\beta  & =2 \\\\ \\beta  & =0.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting into $\\alpha = -3\\beta,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\alpha = -3(0.1) = -0.3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p = - 0.3 \\cos 2x + 0.1  \\sin 2x,\n$$\n\nand the general solution is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={y}_{c}+{y}_{p} \\\\  & =A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{-2x}-0.3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+0.1\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186088951 :tutorial/content "Given a second-order ODE with sinusoidal forcing $f(x) = a \\cos \\omega x + b \\sin \\omega x,$ if $\\cos \\omega x$ or $\\sin \\omega x$ *is* a term in the complementary solution, then $a \\cos \\omega x + b \\sin \\omega x$ *cannot* be a particular solution. Instead, we assume that a particular solution takes the form\n\n$$\ny_p = x(\\alpha \\cos \\omega x + \\beta  \\sin \\omega x).\n$$\n\nFor example, let's find the solution to the following ODE:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} + y= 4 \\cos x.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution by solving the associated homogeneous ODE. The characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\text{i},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso $\\lambda = \\textrm{i}$ or $\\lambda =  -\\textrm{i}.$ Consequently, the complimentary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c = A\\cos x + B \\sin x.\n$$\n\nSecond, we find a particular solution of the inhomogeneous ODE. We see that the right-hand side is $4 \\cos x$ and $\\cos x$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form\n\n$$\ny_p = x (\\alpha \\cos x + \\beta \\sin x),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x} & =(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x), \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =2(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute the derivatives into the original ODE and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x) & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ -2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2\\beta =4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ -2\\alpha =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha = 0$ and $\\beta = 2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p = 2x \\sin{x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087948 :activity/expected-seconds 900.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
